-- Q10 [Threshold, Depolarization & Summation | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub10$Threshold, Depolarization & Summation$sub10$, $qt10$At its axon hillock, a neuron at rest (-70 mV) with a firing threshold of -55 mV simultaneously receives three excitatory inputs producing EPSPs of +9 mV, +8 mV, and +7 mV and two inhibitory inputs producing IPSPs of -3 mV and -1 mV. An investigator records the resulting output. What does the recording show?$qt10$, $op10$[{"label": "A", "text": "The net input drives the membrane well past -55 mV, so the cell fires an unusually large action potential that peaks above the usual value."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The net input drives the membrane to -50 mV, past -55 mV, so the cell fires one action potential whose peak equals that of any other spike."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The net input depolarizes the membrane only to about -60 mV, short of -55 mV, so no action potential is initiated."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Because the largest single EPSP alone reaches only -61 mV, short of -55 mV, the cell fails to fire an action potential."}]$op10$::jsonb, 'B', $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is B because the axon hillock sums all inputs into one net membrane voltage, and that net is what is compared to threshold. The EPSPs add to +24 mV and the IPSPs subtract -4 mV, for a net depolarization of +20 mV; starting from -70 mV this brings Vm to -50 mV, which is past the -55 mV threshold, so voltage-gated Na+ channels open and an action potential is initiated (\"It is the net change in postsynaptic membrane voltage that determines whether the postsynaptic cell has reached its threshold of excitation needed to fire an action potential\"). Because action potentials are all-or-none, the spike's peak is fixed regardless of how far past threshold the input pushed the membrane, since \"A stronger stimulus, which might depolarize the membrane well past threshold, will not make a 'bigger' action potential\" and \"All action potentials peak at the same voltage.\" (Choice A) The cell does fire, but this violates the all-or-none rule by scaling spike amplitude with stimulus strength, which the text explicitly denies. (Choice B) Correct: the summed net (+20 mV) carries Vm to -50 mV, crossing -55 mV and triggering one all-or-none spike of standard peak. (Choice C) This reflects a miscount of the EPSP/IPSP sum that lands just below threshold; the actual net of +20 mV reaches -50 mV, which is above -55 mV. (Choice D) This judges only the largest single EPSP rather than the summed net, but integration at the axon hillock combines all inputs into one membrane voltage before the threshold comparison. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires the student to compute the net summed input from numeric EPSP and IPSP values, predict whether threshold is crossed, and apply the all-or-none principle to the resulting spike's amplitude.$ex10$, 'hard', '3A', $cc10$The Action Potential$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
