UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a10C$Plausible miscount of the EPSP/IPSP sum that lands just below threshold, predicting no firing$a10C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a10D$Evaluates the largest single EPSP against threshold instead of summing all inputs into one net membrane voltage at the axon hillock$a10D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization | easy | Skill 1 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub11$Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization$sub11$, $qt11$A neuron sitting at its resting membrane potential receives a graded depolarizing stimulus, and a researcher records the exact membrane voltage at which its voltage-gated Na+ channels swing open. The recorded value corresponds to which membrane potential?$qt11$, $op11$[{"label": "A", "text": "-70 mV, the resting membrane potential the neuron holds before the stimulus arrives"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A value more negative than -70 mV, reached only after the membrane hyperpolarizes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "About -55 mV, the threshold the depolarizing membrane must reach"}, {"label": "D", "text": "About +30 mV, the peak voltage at the top of depolarization"}]$op11$::jsonb, 'C', $ex11$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because the voltage-gated Na+ channels open only once the depolarizing membrane reaches the threshold of about -55 mV. OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e states that the stimulus-driven channels "help the cell to depolarize from -70 mV to -55 mV. Once the membrane reaches that voltage, the voltage-gated Na+ channels open. This is what is known as the threshold," and OpenStax Biology 2e confirms a "threshold potential (-55 mV)" at which "the sodium channels open." Any depolarization that fails to bring the membrane to -55 mV does not reach threshold and does not open these channels, while the activation gate specifically "opens when the membrane potential crosses -55 mV." (Choice A) -70 mV is the resting potential, where the activation gate is still closed and no voltage-gated Na+ channels have opened. (Choice B) A voltage more negative than -70 mV is hyperpolarization, which moves the membrane away from threshold rather than toward channel opening. (Choice C) About -55 mV is correct, because this is the threshold the depolarizing membrane must reach for the voltage-gated Na+ channels to open. (Choice D) About +30 mV is the peak of depolarization reached after the channels are already open and Na+ has rushed in, not the voltage that triggers their opening. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the specific membrane voltage—threshold at about -55 mV—at which voltage-gated Na+ channels open.$ex11$, 'easy', '3A', $cc11$The Action Potential$cc11$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a11A$Confusing the resting potential (-70 mV) with the threshold voltage that opens the channels$a11A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a11B$Reversing depolarization-to-threshold into hyperpolarization below rest opening the channels$a11B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a11D$Substituting the +30 mV peak of depolarization for the threshold that triggers channel opening$a11D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization | medium | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub12$Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization$sub12$, $qt12$At the axon hillock of a neuron, a stimulus depolarizes the membrane to its threshold and voltage-gated Na+ channels snap open while the cell interior is still around -55 mV. A student is asked to explain why Na+ floods into the cell so rapidly at this instant.$qt12$, $op12$[{"label": "A", "text": "The Na+/K+ pump actively transports Na+ inward through the newly opened voltage-gated channels, using ATP to force the cation into the cell."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its concentration gradient pushes Na+ inward, but the membrane voltage opposes that entry the entire time the channels stay open."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The electrical gradient alone pulls Na+ inward, because Na+ is actually more concentrated inside the cell than in the extracellular fluid."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both its concentration gradient and the electrical attraction of the negative interior drive Na+ inward through the open channels."}]$op12$::jsonb, 'D', $ex12$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because at threshold two forces act on Na+ in the same direction: its chemical concentration gradient and the electrical gradient. The text states that "the concentration of Na+ is higher outside the cell than inside the cell by a factor of 10, ions will rush into the cell that are driven largely by the concentration gradient," and adds that "the electrical gradient also plays a role, as negative proteins below the membrane attract the sodium ion." Because the interior sits near -55 mV (still negative) when the channels open, the cell's negative charge attracts the positively charged cation inward even as the 10-fold outside-to-inside concentration difference pushes it in, so both forces reinforce each other. (Choice A) is wrong because the Na+/K+ pump is an ATP-driven active transporter that moves Na+ outward against its gradient to maintain that gradient; it does not pump ions through passive voltage-gated channels. (Choice B) is a partial truth: the concentration gradient does push Na+ in, but the membrane voltage does not oppose entry the whole time — while the interior is negative it actively attracts Na+, so the electrical force aids entry rather than opposing it. (Choice C) reverses the concentration relationship: Na+ is higher outside the cell, not inside, so it is not driven by the electrical gradient alone. (Choice D) is correct because both the concentration gradient and the electrical attraction of the negative interior drive Na+ inward. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the dual electrochemical driving forces to a specific voltage condition (-55 mV) to predict the direction and cause of Na+ flux rather than recalling a single fact.$ex12$, 'medium', '3A', $cc12$The Action Potential$cc12$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a12A$Confusing the active Na+/K+ pump with passive channel-mediated flux during depolarization$a12A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a12B$Crediting only the concentration gradient and wrongly treating the membrane voltage as opposing Na+ entry throughout$a12B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a12C$Reversing the Na+ concentration gradient (inside higher than outside) and attributing entry to the electrical force alone$a12C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub13$Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization$sub13$, $qt13$A neurophysiologist records from a single axon and freezes the membrane the instant the voltage tracing reaches its peak at about +35 mV. She then asks a student to identify the conformational state of the voltage-gated Na+ channels at that exact moment.$qt13$, $op13$[{"label": "A", "text": "Inactivated, with the inactivation gate shut so Na+ can no longer enter despite the membrane still being depolarized."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Closed at rest, with the activation gate shut just as it is when the membrane sits below the threshold voltage."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fully open and still carrying maximal Na+ current, which is what holds the voltage at its peak value."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Reset to the ready-to-open resting conformation, having already de-inactivated after the depolarization."}]$op13$::jsonb, 'A', $ex13$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is A because the voltage-gated Na+ channel's inactivation gate closes in step with the peak of depolarization, so at +35 mV the channel is in the inactivated state and admits no further Na+ even though the membrane remains depolarized. The A&P text states, "Timed with the peak of depolarization, the inactivation gate closes. During repolarization, no more sodium can enter the cell," and later, "Because voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated at the peak of the depolarization, they cannot be opened again for a brief time." OpenStax Biology 2e adds that after activation these channels "become inactivated for a brief period and will no longer open in response to a signal." Inactivation is a distinct closed conformation (gated by the inactivation gate), not the same as the resting closed state and not the open conducting state. (Choice A) is correct: at the peak the inactivation gate is shut, blocking Na+ entry. (Choice B) is wrong because at rest "the activation gate is closed and the inactivation gate is open," whereas at the peak the activation gate has already opened and it is the inactivation gate that is now shut, so this is a different state. (Choice C) is wrong because the channel is being inactivated, not conducting maximally; the peak reflects inactivating Na+ channels and opening K+ channels, not sustained Na+ influx. (Choice D) is wrong because the inactivation gate re-opens (de-inactivates) only later, after the membrane repolarizes past -55 mV, making "the channel ready to start the whole process over again," not at the peak. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires mapping a specific membrane-voltage condition onto the correct channel conformation and ruling out states that occur at other points in the cycle.$ex13$, 'medium', '3A', $cc13$The Action Potential$cc13$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a13B$Confusing the inactivated peak state with the resting closed state (activation gate shut)$a13B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a13C$Treating the peak as sustained maximal Na+ conduction rather than inactivation$a13C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a13D$Applying the later de-inactivation/reset step to the moment of peak depolarization$a13D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub14$Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization$sub14$, $qt14$A researcher applies a marine toxin to an axon that locks the inactivation gate of every voltage-gated Na+ channel in the open position, so these gates can no longer close on their normal sub-millisecond timescale, while leaving the activation gates and the voltage-gated K+ channels untouched. After the membrane is driven past threshold and the activation gates open, what does the researcher MOST likely observe about the membrane potential?$qt14$, $op14$[{"label": "A", "text": "The membrane repolarizes back toward -70 mV faster than in an untreated axon because the open Na+ channels speed the return to rest."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The membrane remains abnormally depolarized and fails to return promptly toward rest because Na+ continues to enter through channels that never inactivate."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The membrane never reaches threshold, so no action potential is initiated and the potential stays at the -70 mV resting value."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The membrane peaks normally at about +30 mV and then hyperpolarizes far below the usual overshoot because of the extra Na+ that entered."}]$op14$::jsonb, 'B', $ex14$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is B because closing of the Na+ channel's inactivation gate is what normally stops Na+ entry and allows repolarization, so a toxin that holds that gate open lets Na+ keep flooding in and prevents a prompt return toward rest. The text explains that the voltage-gated Na+ channel has an inactivation gate "which closes after a specific period of time" and that, once it has closed, "During repolarization, no more sodium can enter the cell"; this gate closing is precisely the step that ends depolarization on the Na+ side. If the gate cannot close, the channels never inactivate, Na+ continues to rush into the cell down its gradient, and the membrane stays driven toward the depolarized side rather than repolarizing. The K+ channels are intact and will still open, but they cannot promptly restore a normal resting potential while a sustained inward Na+ current opposes them, so the membrane sits abnormally depolarized. (Choice A) is reversed: open Na+ channels carry an inward, depolarizing current, so they oppose rather than accelerate the return toward -70 mV. (Choice B) is correct because the persistently open channels keep Na+ entering, the very condition the text says must stop ("no more sodium can enter the cell") for repolarization to proceed. (Choice C) is wrong because the toxin acts on the inactivation gate, not the activation gate; the scenario states the membrane is driven past threshold and the activation gates open, so an action potential is initiated. (Choice D) is wrong because the text ties the hyperpolarizing overshoot to continued K+ efflux, not to extra Na+ entry; sustained Na+ influx would oppose, not deepen, hyperpolarization, and the membrane would not cleanly peak and then dip below rest. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting, from a described experimental perturbation, how disabling one specific gating step alters the downstream behavior of the membrane potential.$ex14$, 'hard', '3A', $cc14$The Action Potential$cc14$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a14A$Treats an inward, depolarizing Na+ current as if it sped repolarization, reversing the actual direction of the channel's effect on Vm.$a14A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a14C$Confuses the inactivation gate with the activation gate, wrongly concluding threshold can never be reached and no action potential fires.$a14C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a14D$Correctly recalls the +30 mV peak and the hyperpolarizing overshoot but misattributes the overshoot to extra Na+ entry instead of continued K+ efflux.$a14D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub15$Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels & Depolarization$sub15$, $qt15$A neurophysiologist drives a neuron to fire, then delivers a second stimulus stronger than the first at the exact instant the membrane reaches the +30 mV peak of depolarization. No second action potential is generated by this stimulus. Which prediction BEST explains why the neuron cannot fire again at this moment?$qt15$, $op15$[{"label": "A", "text": "No spike fires because the voltage-gated K+ channels are still closed, so the membrane cannot first repolarize to support a new action potential."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A second, larger spike fires at once because the stronger stimulus forces the inactivated voltage-gated Na+ channels back open."}, {"label": "C", "text": "No spike fires because the voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated and cannot reopen until the membrane potential returns near rest to de-inactivate them."}, {"label": "D", "text": "No spike fires because the Na+/K+ pump has not yet restored the resting ion concentration gradients needed for another action potential."}]$op15$::jsonb, 'C', $ex15$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because at the peak of depolarization the voltage-gated Na+ channels are in their inactivated state, and their inactivation gate only re-opens after the membrane repolarizes back toward rest. The text states that "Because voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated at the peak of the depolarization, they cannot be opened again for a brief time—the absolute refractory period," so no stimulus, however strong, can reopen them at +30 mV. The inactivation gate re-opens only "When the membrane potential passes -55 mV again" and the channel returns "to its resting conformation (less than -55 mV)," at which point a new action potential becomes possible; OpenStax Biology 2e likewise notes the Na+ channels "will return to their resting state... ready to open again if the membrane potential again exceeds the threshold." This voltage-dependent de-inactivation, not the pump or the K+ channels, is what defines the absolute refractory period. (Choice A) is wrong because the voltage-gated K+ channels actually open at the peak—"voltage-gated K+ channels open just as the voltage-gated Na+ channels are being inactivated"—so they are not the limiting factor. (Choice B) is wrong because action potentials are "all or none" and "a stronger stimulus... will not make a 'bigger' action potential," and during the absolute phase "another action potential will not start... because of the inactivation gate," which no stimulus can override. (Choice C) is correct because Na+-channel inactivation, reversed only by repolarization near rest, is the direct cause of the absolute refractory period at the depolarization peak. (Choice D) is wrong because, although the Na/K pump "help[s] to maintain the resting potential" over time, a single action potential moves too few ions to deplete the gradients, and de-inactivation is gated by voltage rather than by pump-restored concentrations. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the mechanism of voltage-gated Na+-channel inactivation to predict the outcome of a specific timed stimulus and to rule out plausible but incorrect causes.$ex15$, 'hard', '3A', $cc15$The Action Potential$cc15$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
