SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a15A$Misorders the repolarization step by claiming K+ channels are still closed at the peak, when they open as Na+ channels inactivate.$a15A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a15B$Believes a sufficiently strong (supra-threshold) stimulus can force inactivated Na+ channels open and produce a graded, larger spike, violating all-or-none and refractoriness.$a15B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a15D$Correctly notes the Na+/K+ pump maintains gradients but wrongly makes pump-restored concentration the gate on re-firing, when voltage-dependent de-inactivation is the real cause.$a15D$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization | easy | Skill 1 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub16$Repolarization & Hyperpolarization$sub16$, $qt16$During the repolarizing phase of a neuron's action potential, voltage-gated channels permit a specific ion to cross the membrane and return the voltage toward -70 mV. A student is asked to name the ion and the direction it travels to produce this repolarization.$qt16$, $op16$[{"label": "A", "text": "Sodium ions (Na+) move out of the cell, carrying positive charge out and returning the potential toward rest."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Potassium ions (K+) move into the cell down their gradient, adding positive charge to the interior."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chloride ions (Cl-) move into the cell, adding negative charge to the interior to restore the resting potential."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Potassium ions (K+) move out of the cell down their gradient, carrying positive charge out and returning the potential toward rest."}]$op16$::jsonb, 'D', $ex16$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because repolarization is driven by potassium ions exiting the cell down their concentration gradient, which removes positive charge from the interior and drives the membrane voltage back toward its resting value of -70 mV. As the membrane reaches the peak near +30 mV, voltage-gated K+ channels open, and because K+ is far more concentrated inside the cell than outside, K+ flows outward; the text states that "as K+ starts to leave the cell, taking a positive charge with it, the membrane potential begins to move back toward its resting voltage. This is called repolarization." Losing positive charge from inside makes the interior more negative again, completing the return toward -70 mV. (Choice A) Na+ does carry positive charge, but Na+ is more concentrated outside the cell and moves into the cell during depolarization, not out during repolarization, so it is the wrong ion identity for the repolarizing efflux. (Choice B) K+ is the correct ion, but it moves out of (not into) the cell; the text specifies K+ "leaving the cell," so adding positive charge to the interior reverses the actual direction. (Choice C) Cl- entering the cell is a real hyperpolarizing event the text lists alongside K+ efflux, but it is not the current that drives repolarization of the action potential here, which is carried by potassium leaving. (Choice D) K+ moving out of the cell down its gradient carries positive charge out and returns the membrane toward -70 mV, which is exactly the mechanism of repolarization. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established identity and direction of the ion movement (K+ efflux) that mediates the repolarizing phase of the action potential.$ex16$, 'easy', '3A', $cc16$The Action Potential$cc16$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
