-- Q17 [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization | easy | Skill 1 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub17$Repolarization & Hyperpolarization$sub17$, $qt17$During a single action potential in a neuron, the voltage-gated K+ channels are slow to close, so K+ keeps leaving the cell even after the membrane has dropped back through -70 mV, driving the membrane potential briefly to a value more negative than the resting potential. The phase in which the membrane is momentarily more negative than -70 mV is named:$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "depolarization, the phase in which the membrane potential moves from -70 mV toward zero as Na+ enters the cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "repolarization, the phase in which the membrane potential returns from +30 mV back to the -70 mV resting value"}, {"label": "C", "text": "hyperpolarization, the phase in which the membrane potential overshoots below -70 mV as K+ continues to leave the cell"}, {"label": "D", "text": "threshold, the -55 mV value at which the voltage-gated Na+ channels open to begin the action potential"}]$op17$::jsonb, 'C', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because hyperpolarization is the name of the phase in which the membrane potential briefly becomes more negative than the -70 mV resting value. As repolarization returns the membrane toward rest, the voltage-gated K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing, so K+ continues to leave the cell; because K+ reaches equilibrium at a voltage below -70 mV, the membrane overshoots the resting value, producing the hyperpolarizing overshoot before the non-gated channels and the Na+/K+ pump restore the resting potential. (Choice A) Depolarization is incorrect because it is the opposite-direction phase, in which Na+ entry drives the membrane from -70 mV up toward +30 mV, not below rest. (Choice B) Repolarization is incorrect because, although it is the phase immediately preceding the overshoot and does involve K+ leaving the cell, it names only the return of the membrane voltage back toward the -70 mV resting value, not the dip below it. (Choice C) Hyperpolarization is correct because it specifically names the brief overshoot below -70 mV caused by the slow closing of the voltage-gated K+ channels. (Choice D) Threshold is incorrect because it is a fixed -55 mV trigger point at which voltage-gated Na+ channels open to start the action potential, not a phase in which the membrane sits below rest. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling and identifying the defined phase of the action potential associated with a membrane potential more negative than rest.$ex17$, 'easy', '3A', $cc17$The Action Potential$cc17$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
