-- Q18 [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub18$Repolarization & Hyperpolarization$sub18$, $qt18$A researcher applies a drug that selectively blocks voltage-gated K+ channels at the exact moment a neuron's membrane potential reaches the +30 mV peak of its action potential. Assuming all other channels behave normally, the researcher predicts that the membrane will respond in which way immediately after the peak?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "The membrane potential lingers near its positive peak instead of falling back toward the resting value."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The membrane reaches threshold and depolarizes faster because Na+ no longer competes with outgoing K+ during the rising phase."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cell hyperpolarizes more deeply below -70 mV because trapped K+ builds an even larger outward driving force."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Repolarization proceeds normally because the Na+/K+ ATPase pumps the positive charge back out and resets the voltage by itself."}]$op18$::jsonb, 'A', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is A because repolarization is driven by K+ leaving the cell through voltage-gated K+ channels, so blocking those channels removes the outward current that would otherwise pull the voltage back down, leaving the membrane stuck near its positive peak. The text states that "As K+ starts to leave the cell, taking a positive charge with it, the membrane potential begins to move back toward its resting voltage. This is called repolarization," and that at the peak "voltage-gated K+ channels open, allowing K+ to leave the cell. As K+ ions leave the cell, the membrane potential once again becomes negative and repolarizes." If that K+ efflux is prevented, the positive charge brought in during depolarization is not carried back out, so the voltage cannot return toward -70 mV and instead remains depolarized. (Choice A) is correct because eliminating K+ efflux removes the only current driving the falling phase, so the membrane stays near +30 mV. (Choice B) is wrong because blocking K+ channels affects the falling phase, not the Na+-driven rising phase; the depolarizing rise to +30 mV is produced by voltage-gated Na+ channels opening at threshold, independent of K+ flow. (Choice C) is wrong because the hyperpolarizing overshoot below -70 mV is itself caused by K+ continuing "to leave the cell for a short while," so blocking K+ exit prevents the overshoot rather than deepening it. (Choice D) is wrong because, although the Na+/K+ pump and non-gated channels eventually restore the resting state, the pump is far too slow to repolarize a single spike, which normally takes only about 2 milliseconds and depends on rapid K+ efflux. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the downstream consequence of a pharmacological perturbation by tracing how loss of K+ efflux disrupts the repolarization mechanism.$ex18$, 'medium', '3A', $cc18$The Action Potential$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
