SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a18B$Attributing the falling-phase effect of K+ channels to the Na+-driven rising phase$a18B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a18C$Treating blocked K+ exit as deepening hyperpolarization when it actually prevents the overshoot$a18C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a18D$Crediting the slow Na+/K+ ATPase with repolarizing a single fast spike$a18D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub19$Repolarization & Hyperpolarization$sub19$, $qt19$During the falling phase of an action potential, a neuron's membrane potential briefly dips below its resting value of -70 mV before settling back to rest. A student is asked to explain why repolarization undershoots into a transient hyperpolarization instead of halting cleanly at -70 mV. Which explanation correctly accounts for this brief overshoot?$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "The Na+/K+ ATPase momentarily exceeds its 3 Na+ out / 2 K+ in ratio, expelling extra positive charge that drives the interior below the resting voltage."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The voltage-gated K+ channels close with a delay, so K+ continues to exit even after -70 mV is reached, briefly carrying the interior below the resting voltage."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The voltage-gated Na+ channels briefly reopen and carry a few Na+ back out of the cell, drawing the interior below the resting voltage."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Leakage Cl- channels open at the peak and let Cl- rush inward, adding negative charge that carries the interior below the resting voltage."}]$op19$::jsonb, 'B', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is B because the voltage-gated K+ channels are slow to close once the membrane has repolarized, so outward K+ flow persists past -70 mV and transiently drives the potential more negative than rest. The text states that during repolarization the membrane "actually overshoots that value" and that "Those K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing, accounting for this short overshoot," with potassium continuing "to leave the cell for a short while" so that "the membrane potential becomes more negative, resulting in the hyperpolarizing overshoot." A parallel passage notes the undershoot "is the result of the slow closing of the K+ channels." Because potassium's equilibrium lies below -70 mV, continued K+ efflux during this delay pulls the interior past resting potential before the channels finally close. (Choice A) is wrong because the Na+/K+ ATPase moves a fixed three Na+ out for every two K+ in and does not vary or overshoot that stoichiometry to cause hyperpolarization. (Choice B) is correct because delayed closing of the voltage-gated K+ channels sustains K+ efflux past -70 mV, producing the brief undershoot. (Choice C) is wrong because during repolarization the voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated and admit no further sodium, and when open they carry sodium inward, not outward. (Choice D) is wrong because although Cl- influx can hyperpolarize a membrane in graded-potential signaling, the action-potential undershoot is produced by continued K+ efflux, not chloride entry. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires reasoning from the observed undershoot back to the channel-kinetics mechanism that produces it, distinguishing the true cause from plausible but incorrect ionic explanations.$ex19$, 'medium', '3A', $cc19$The Action Potential$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a19A$Attributing hyperpolarization to variable pump output rather than to delayed K+ channel kinetics; the pump's stoichiometry is fixed at 3 Na+/2 K+.$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a19C$Reversing the direction and state of Na+ channels: they are inactivated during repolarization and carry inward (not outward) current when open.$a19C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a19D$Importing a real but context-mismatched mechanism: Cl- influx hyperpolarizes in graded-potential signaling, but does not produce the action-potential undershoot.$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Repolarization & Hyperpolarization | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub20$Repolarization & Hyperpolarization$sub20$, $qt20$A researcher injects a neuron with ouabain, which fully blocks the Na+/K+ ATPase but leaves the voltage-gated Na+ and K+ channels and the K+ leakage channels intact, then immediately triggers one suprathreshold stimulus. The neuron is asked to predict what happens to the falling phase of that single action potential and to the cell's ability to fire repeatedly afterward.$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "Repolarization fails immediately because the Na+/K+ ATPase is the protein that pumps the positive charge back out to drive the voltage down from +30 mV."}, {"label": "B", "text": "That single spike cannot repolarize past -55 mV because the pump must first remove the Na+ that entered during the rising phase before the voltage can fall."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The hyperpolarizing overshoot is lost while ordinary repolarization to -70 mV is preserved because the pump carries the voltage the extra distance below rest."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Repolarization and the hyperpolarizing overshoot occur normally for that spike, but the dissipated Na+ and K+ gradients are not rebuilt, so repeated firing progressively runs them down."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because the falling phase of an action potential is driven by passive K+ efflux through voltage-gated K+ channels, not by the Na/K ATPase, so a single spike repolarizes and overshoots normally even when the pump is blocked; the pump's job is to slowly restore the dissipated concentration gradients over many cycles. The text states that as K+ leaves the cell "the membrane potential begins to move back toward its resting voltage. This is called repolarization," and that the brief overshoot occurs because "those K+ channels are slightly delayed in closing." The Na/K pump instead "basically maintains those concentration gradients," and after the repolarizing phase "K+ leakage channels and the Na+/K+ pump ensure that the ions return to their original locations" — a restorative role spread across many spikes, so blocking it only manifests as a progressive run-down with repeated firing. Because the Na+ inactivation gate closes during depolarization, no pump action is needed to halt Na+ entry or permit the fall. (Choice A) is wrong because the pump does not repolarize the membrane; passive K+ efflux drives the voltage down from +30 mV. (Choice B) is wrong because repolarization proceeds with the Na+ inactivation gate already closed, and clearing accumulated Na+ over many spikes is a separate, slower process than repolarizing one spike. (Choice C) is wrong because the hyperpolarizing overshoot comes from the delayed closing of the voltage-gated K+ channels, not from the pump. (Choice D) is correct because the immediate falling phase and overshoot are passive K+-driven events that occur normally, while only gradient restoration — and thus sustained repeated firing — depends on the pump. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the differential consequence of a specific pump block on a single spike versus repeated firing by separating the passive ionic basis of repolarization from the active restorative role of the Na/K ATPase.$ex20$, 'hard', '3A', $cc20$The Action Potential$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a20A$Assigns the Na/K ATPase the repolarizing job that actually belongs to passive K+ efflux$a20A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a20B$Conflates slow clearing of accumulated Na+ over many spikes with repolarizing one spike$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a20C$Correctly isolates the overshoot but mis-attributes it to the pump rather than to delayed K+-channel closing$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Refractory Periods | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub21$Refractory Periods$sub21$, $qt21$A researcher delivers a single suprathreshold stimulus to an axon, then delivers a second, even stronger stimulus 0.5 ms later, while the membrane is still in its absolute refractory period. The researcher predicts whether the second stimulus will evoke another action potential.$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "No second action potential occurs, because the voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated and cannot reopen."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A second action potential occurs, because the stronger stimulus exceeds the elevated threshold of the membrane."}, {"label": "C", "text": "No second action potential occurs until the voltage-gated K+ channels have closed and restored the resting potential."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A second action potential occurs, because the Na+/K+ pump has already restored the Na+ concentration gradient."}]$op21$::jsonb, 'A', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is A because during the absolute refractory period the voltage-gated Na+ channels are in their inactivated state, so no stimulus of any strength can reopen them to trigger a second action potential. The OpenStax A&P text states that "during the absolute phase, another action potential will not start. This is because of the inactivation gate of the voltage-gated Na+ channel," and that these channels "are inactivated at the peak of the depolarization, they cannot be opened again for a brief time—the absolute refractory period." OpenStax Biology adds that once the channels close "and cannot be opened," the neuron "cannot produce another action potential because its sodium channels will not open." Stimulus strength is irrelevant here precisely because the gating, not the threshold, is the barrier. (Choice A) is correct because Na+ channel inactivation makes a second action potential impossible regardless of stimulus strength during the absolute period. (Choice B) is wrong because an elevated threshold that a stronger stimulus can overcome describes the relative, not the absolute, refractory period; during the absolute period no stimulus strength suffices. (Choice C) is wrong because reopening the Na+ channels depends on their return to the resting conformation, not simply on K+ channels closing. (Choice D) is wrong because recovery of excitability depends on Na+ channels returning from inactivation, not on the Na+/K+ pump restoring the ion gradient. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established mechanism that Na+ channel inactivation defines the absolute refractory period.$ex21$, 'easy', '3A', $cc21$The Action Potential$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a21B$Treating 'absolute' as merely 'harder to trigger' so a sufficiently strong stimulus could still fire$a21B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a21C$Applying the relative-refractory-period requirement (K+ channels closed/resting restored) to the absolute period$a21C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a21D$Invoking the Na+/K+ pump restoring the gradient instead of Na+ channel inactivation$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Refractory Periods | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub22$Refractory Periods$sub22$, $qt22$During the relative refractory period of a neuron, the inactivation gates of the voltage-gated Na+ channels have already returned to their resting conformation, yet voltage-gated K+ channels remain open and K+ continues to leave the cell. A researcher finds that a stimulus of normal strength fails to trigger a new action potential at this moment, and only a larger-than-normal stimulus succeeds.$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "A larger stimulus is required because the voltage-gated Na+ channels remain inactivated and only a strong stimulus can force their inactivation gates open."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A larger stimulus is required because K+ is still leaving the cell, so incoming Na+ only counteracts the ongoing hyperpolarization instead of depolarizing the membrane to threshold."}, {"label": "C", "text": "A larger stimulus is required because the Na+/K+ pump is exporting Na+ faster than it can enter, so most of the stimulus current is removed before it acts."}, {"label": "D", "text": "A larger stimulus is required because the threshold voltage itself has shifted to a more positive value during this period, raising the depolarization needed to fire."}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is B because, once the Na+ inactivation gates have reset, the only remaining obstacle to firing is the continued efflux of K+, which opposes the depolarizing effect of any Na+ that enters. The text explains that after the Na+ channel returns to its resting conformation, "a new action potential could be started, but only by a stronger stimulus than the one that initiated the current action potential. This is because of the flow of K+ out of the cell. Because that ion is rushing out, any Na+ that tries to enter will not depolarize the cell, but will only keep the cell from hyperpolarizing." In other words, incoming Na+ first has to offset the outward K+ current and the resulting hyperpolarized starting voltage before it can push the membrane toward threshold, so a greater-than-normal stimulus is needed. (Choice A) is wrong because Na+ channels still being inactivated defines the absolute refractory period, not the relative one; the stem specifies the inactivation gates have already reset. (Choice B) is correct because ongoing K+ efflux means entering Na+ merely counteracts hyperpolarization rather than depolarizing the cell to threshold. (Choice C) is wrong because the relative refractory period arises from voltage-gated K+ efflux, not from the comparatively slow Na+/K+ pump, which restores ion gradients over a longer timescale. (Choice D) is wrong because threshold (about -55 mV) does not move; the membrane simply starts from a more negative, hyperpolarized voltage, increasing the gap that must be crossed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires inferring, from the stated channel and ion conditions, why the same threshold becomes harder to reach rather than recalling a single defined term.$ex22$, 'medium', '3A', $cc22$The Action Potential$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a22A$Attributes the relative refractory period to Na+ channel inactivation, which actually defines the absolute refractory period$a22A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a22C$Blames the Na+/K+ pump for the difficulty instead of ongoing voltage-gated K+ efflux$a22C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a22D$Invents a shifting threshold value instead of recognizing a more-negative starting membrane potential$a22D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Refractory Periods | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Nervous System', $sub23$Refractory Periods$sub23$, $qt23$A neurophysiologist artificially depolarizes a patch in the middle of a healthy unmyelinated axon and watches the resulting impulse spread toward both ends, yet finds it dies out on the side that had just been firing while continuing normally toward the axon terminal. The membrane that just fired cannot be re-excited by the backward-spreading depolarization.$qt23$, $op23$[{"label": "A", "text": "The action potential moves one way because voltage-gated Na+ channels are present only in the membrane ahead of the impulse and are absent from the membrane it has already crossed."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The action potential moves one way because the Na+/K+ pump immediately restores the resting potential in the membrane behind the impulse, blocking any re-excitation there."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The action potential moves one way because the Na+ channels in the just-fired membrane behind the impulse are inactivated, so the backward-spreading depolarization cannot reopen them."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The action potential moves one way because the myelin insulating the trailing membrane prevents backward current flow into the region that has already fired."}]$op23$::jsonb, 'C', $ex23$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is C because the membrane just behind the advancing impulse is in its absolute refractory period: its voltage-gated Na+ channels were inactivated at the peak of depolarization and cannot reopen for a brief interval, so depolarization that spreads backward into that region has no effect and only the forward, resting membrane can fire. The text states that "voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated at the peak of the depolarization, they cannot be opened again for a brief time—the absolute refractory period. Because of this, depolarization spreading back toward previously opened channels has no effect" (A&P 2e), and that during the refractory period the neuron "cannot produce another action potential because its sodium channels will not open" (Biology 2e). This Na+-channel inactivation, not channel distribution, the pump, or myelin, is what enforces one-way conduction toward the axon terminals. (Choice A) is wrong because voltage-gated Na+ channels are distributed along the axon membrane and open as depolarization spreads, so the trailing membrane does contain channels—they are merely inactivated, not absent. (Choice B) is wrong because the Na+/K+ pump only slowly restores ion gradients and does not act fast enough to reset the membrane behind the impulse; the immediate block on re-excitation comes from Na+-channel inactivation, not the pump. (Choice C) is correct because inactivated Na+ channels in the just-fired membrane cannot be reopened by the backward-spreading depolarization, so the impulse can only continue forward. (Choice D) is wrong because this axon is unmyelinated, and even in myelinated axons directionality is enforced by the refractory period rather than by insulation of the trailing membrane. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires interpreting an experimental observation of bidirectional spread and predicting, from the mechanism of Na+-channel inactivation, why only the forward direction propagates.$ex23$, 'medium', '3A', $cc23$The Action Potential$cc23$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
